<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Republic of the Congo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Congo-Brazzaville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Republic of the Congo is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> as its capital. As the French colony of Middle Congo it was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, and it became independent on 15 August 1960. It was a Marxist-Leninist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> from 1969 to 1991 and depends heavily on oil exports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo.jpg" alt="Republic of the Congo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Loango was created by Kongo people on the Atlantic coast north of the more powerful Kingdom of Kongo, in what is now the western <b>Republic of the Congo</b>, southern Gabon and Cabinda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingship survives as a ceremonial office in the <b>Republic of the Congo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tio">Kingdom of Tio</a></li><li><blockquote>Middle Congo became the autonomous <b>Republic of the Congo</b> in November 1958 under the priest-politician Fulbert Youlou, and gained full independence on 15 August 1960, keeping Brazzaville as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a></li></ul></section>
